    {
      "id": "US-mechanism",
      "kind": "conditional_mechanism",
      "text": "Once civilizational resource networks exist, no place needs to produce every condition of survival and development on its own. Food, energy, materials, technology, and professional capability can move across regions, allowing different places to become conditions for one another through specialization and exchange. Cities can support populations far larger than nearby land could directly feed, and highly specialized industries can exist because resource networks recombine the capabilities of many places into larger circulation. Material civilizational capacity therefore comes from sustained relations rather than a single center owning everything.",
